e local master is f2c0e7b but origin/master is 674894f9 (local master is behind; the worktree doesn't track local master though).\n\nImportant: `refs/heads/forest/if-critic-silent-canopy-config/fail-loud-default-config` points to 0b6189b97... (an OLD sha!), while `refs/remotes/origin/forest/if-critic-silent-canopy-config/fail-loud-default-config` points to 6bb6ccb7. So the local branch is stale. But I should rely on origin refs (ls-remote origin).\n\nSo branch tips on origin:\n1. e15fcacd (mocked-checkouts) - verdict exists\n2. b2d365b7 (docs) - request, no verdict\n3. ca9e2379 (expand-checkout) - verdict exists\n4. 23f35a38 (log-drawer) - request, no verdict\n5. fbb9338e (reconcile) - verdict exists\n6. 0f61b400 (tab-visibility) - verdict exists\n7. c203dc91 (audit-divergence) - verdict exists\n8. d2928e01 (empty-inventory) - request, no verdict\n9. 3a4a2ee4 (initial-collection) - request, no verdict\n10. 674894f9 (route-param) - verdict exists (and is master)\n11. 7a8b651f (log-sanitize) - request, no verdict\n12. 6bb6ccb7 (silent-config) - request, no verdict\n\nSo 6 candidates with request and no verdict: b2d365b7, 23f35a38, d2928e01, 3a4a2ee4, 7a8b651f, 6bb6ccb7.\n\nAll are stale (don't contain master 674894f9). Wait, but let me verify each actually. Earlier I checked and all said \"does NOT contain master (stale)\". Let me re-verify because origin/master is 674894f9 and these branches were created before it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Some of these branches might actually be based on a commit that IS an ancestor of master, but master has additional commits on top. Since master is a descendant of them, they are \"stale\" - i.e., they don't contain the current primary ref (master's tip). The rule says the revision SHA must contain the current primary ref (master must be ancestor of the revision). Since master is AHEAD of these branches, master is not their ancestor, so they're stale.\n\nBut wait - actually some branches could be AHEAD of master if they were rebased after master. None of them are; all 6 are stale.\n\nHmm, but then should I review a stale branch and give \"changes\"? The Verifier instructions seem to expect that I might find a stale SHA and report changes. This is a legitimate stop condition: \"stale revisions\" is listed in \"Report ... stale revisions ... with exact evidence.\"\n\nBut actually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe there's a branch that's NOT stale that I'm missing. Let me check the ancestry for ALL origin forest branches against origin/master, including those with verdicts (maybe one was re-requested?).\n\nActually, the candidates are only those with request AND no verdict. Let me also check: is there any request ref whose sha does NOT have a verdict AND whose branch tip contains master? \n\nBranch tips with request and no verdict: 6 candidates, all stale.\n\nSo no approvable revision exists right now. My job is to select ONE and report. The result will be `changes` due to staleness (after running checks and review? Or maybe I should report no-work?).\n\nHmm, let me think about the \"no-work\" condition. The Verifier system prompt says: \"Report no-work, malformed evidence, failed checks, stale revisions, rejected merges, credential exposure, and other unexpected state with exact evidence.\"\n\nSo if all candidate revisions are stale, what should I do? The selection step says \"Select one branch tip with request evidence and no verdict evidence.\" There ARE such branch tips (6 of them). So it's not \"no-work\" - there is work, but the work will result in a \"changes\" verdict due to staleness.\n\nActually wait - let me reconsider. Is it possible that the intended selection is the OLDEST pending request, and the expected outcome is \"changes\" because it's stale, which will signal the fixer to rebase? That seems plausible and is a valid verifier pass.\n\nBut actually, let me reconsider the ancestry more carefully. The rule: \"require the exact SHA to contain the current primary ref: git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/master <sha>\". \n\n`git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/master <sha>` returns true if origin/master is an ancestor of <sha> (i.e., <sha> contains origin/master). For stale branches where master has advanced beyond them, this is false.\n\nSo all 6 candidates are stale. I'll select one and report changes.\n\nBut which one? Let me think about selection ordering. Perhaps I should pick the one that is the \"next\" in queue. Looking at the git history, there's a linear-ish sequence of requests being merged. The most recent merged was route-param-validation (674894f9). Before that, audit-divergence-stale (c203dc91). Before that, 6227bb6f (some request). \n\nActually, the order of requests (by commit in the graph) - let me look at the request refs' underlying commits and their timestamps.\n\nThe pending requests (request refs without verdict):\n- request/6bb6ccb7 → commit 899d885 (top of graph, most recent)\n- request/23f35a38 → commit c8619ab\n- request/3a4a2ee4 → commit d69c709\n- request/d2928e01 → commit d31eb8d\n- request/b2d365b7 → commit eab3ec6\n- request/7a8b651f → commit 89c2033\n\nThese request commits are separate commits (forest request <sha>) that record the request. Their order in the graph (top to bottom) is:\n1. 899d885 (request 6bb6ccb7) - newest\n2. c8619ab (request 23f35a38)\n3. d69c709 (request 3a4a2ee4)\n4. ... \n5. d31eb8d (request d2928e01)\n6. eab3ec6 (request b2d365b7)\n7. 89c2033 (request 7a8b651f) - oldest\n\nHmm, actually the graph is complex. Let me get commit timestamps to determine order.\n\nActually, the selection criterion in the system prompt is just \"Select one branch tip with request evidence and no verdict evidence.\" It doesn't specify a particular ordering. But perhaps there's an implied order - oldest request first (FIFO). Let me check the request commit dates.\n\nLet me also read the request.json files for each candidate to understand what branch and SHA they name, and verify committer requirement